+  /* Compute the Matroska DisplayWidth/DisplayHeight from the pixel aspect
+   * ratio.
+   *
+   * Terminology note: despite their names, the pixel_width / pixel_height
+   * locals here hold the pixel-aspect-ratio (PAR) numerator and denominator
+   * that were read from the "pixel-aspect-ratio" caps field just below. They
+   * are NOT the coded frame dimensions; those are 'width' and 'height'.
+   *
+   * How Matroska stores aspect ratio:
+   *   Matroska has no PAR element. Aspect ratio is expressed through
+   *   DisplayWidth / DisplayHeight together with DisplayUnit. matroskamux
+   *   never writes a DisplayUnit, so it defaults to 0 ("pixels"). In that mode
+   *   the display dimensions describe the frame as it should be presented, and
+   *   consumers recover the pixel aspect ratio from the ratio between the
+   *   display size and the coded (Pixel*) size. matroskademux does exactly
+   *   this, reconstructing:
+   *       PAR = (DisplayWidth  * PixelHeight)
+   *           : (DisplayHeight * PixelWidth)
+   *   (see gst_matroska_demux_add_stream() in matroska-demux.c). Note that only
+   *   the *ratio* of the display dimensions matters to this reconstruction, not
+   *   their absolute magnitude.
+   *
+   * The problem with the previous computation:
+   *   The previous code held one axis at the coded size and stretched the
+   *   other:
+   *       if (par_n > par_d) display = (width * par_n / par_d, height);
+   *       else               display = (width, height * par_d / par_n);
+   *   That integer division rounds whenever the PAR does not divide the coded
+   *   size evenly, which is the common case for anamorphic DVD content. For
+   *   NTSC 16:9 (720x480, PAR 32:27):
+   *       DisplayWidth = 720 * 32 / 27 = 853.33... -> 853  (truncated)
+   *   which reconstructs as PAR 853:720 (~1.1847) instead of the intended
+   *   32:27 (~1.1852) -> DAR 853:480 instead of a clean 16:9. The error is
+   *   ~0.04% (sub-pixel) but is a lossy, non-reversible representation of the
+   *   source aspect ratio.
+   *
+   * The fix:
+   *   Store the exact aspect ratio as a reduced integer fraction instead of a
+   *   rounded pixel count:
+   *       DisplayWidth : DisplayHeight
+   *           = (width * par_n) : (height * par_d)   reduced by their GCD
+   *   Because only the ratio matters (DisplayUnit = pixels), this reconstructs
+   *   the original PAR losslessly. Worked example, NTSC 16:9:
+   *       720*32 : 480*27 = 23040 : 12960  -> /1440 -> 16 : 9
+   *       demux: (16 * 480) : (9 * 720) = 7680 : 6480 -> 32 : 27  (exact)
+   *
+   *   Previous vs new display size, and the PAR each reconstructs to via
+   *   matroskademux, for a range of inputs (checked locally):
+   *
+   *     input (coded, PAR)         previous disp / PAR      new disp / PAR
+   *     720x480  32:27 (NTSC 16:9) 853x480  / 853:720 (bad) 16x9   / 32:27
+   *     720x480   8:9  (NTSC 4:3)  720x540  / 8:9           4x3    / 8:9
+   *     704x480  40:33 (NTSC 16:9) 853x480  / 853:704 (bad) 16x9   / 40:33
+   *     352x480  80:33 (Half-D1)   853x480  / 853:352 (bad) 16x9   / 80:33
+   *     720x576  16:11 (PAL 16:9)  1047x576 / 349:240 (bad) 20x11  / 16:11
+   *     720x576  12:11 (PAL 4:3)   785x576  / 157:144 (bad) 15x11  / 12:11
+   *     480x640   3:4  (portrait)  480x853  / 640:853 (bad) 9x16   / 3:4
+   *     any       1:1  (square)    0x0 (none)               0x0 (none)
+   *
+   * Tradeoff / rationale:
+   *   The reduced form can yield small display dimensions (e.g. 16x9) rather
+   *   than dimensions close to the coded size (e.g. 853x480). Since DisplayUnit
+   *   is pixels, the numbers define the presentation ratio, and matroskademux
+   *   reconstructs from the ratio regardless of magnitude. Preserving the exact
+   *   ratio is preferred here over keeping large pixel counts so that the
+   *   source aspect ratio is not silently altered.
+   *
+   * Square pixels (PAR 1:1) are treated as "no display size" (0/0), matching
+   * the original behaviour; matroskamux additionally strips 1:1 PAR from caps
+   * upstream, so this branch is normally not even reached for square pixels. */
